Add VQE, localization, experiment configurations, evaluation scripts and tests.

2. When is QFlowPilot actually working?
I suggest defining five milestones. Each milestone must pass its tests before moving forward.
Milestone 1 — Basic execution
First target

After Stages 1–3

QFlowPilot can:
- Accept a QAOA Max-Cut workload.
- Build a quantum circuit.
- Execute it using Qiskit Aer.
- Receive measurement counts.
- Evaluate the solution.
Success condition: A complete circuit-to-result execution works without manually running each component.

Milestone 2 — Measurement and quality analysis

After Stages 4–6

QFlowPilot can:
- Analyze circuit characteristics.
- Calculate measurement statistics and uncertainty.
- Evaluate application-level quality.
- Record execution time and resource estimates.
Success condition: Every execution produces a result with quality, statistical and performance measurements.

Milestone 3 — Quality-aware decisions

After Stage 7

QFlowPilot can:
- Select a shot budget.
- Request additional shots when necessary.
- Compare quality against a target.
- Decide whether to continue or use classical fallback.
Success condition: Fixed-shot and adaptive policies execute correctly and their decisions are recorded.

Milestone 4 — Complete runtime

After Stages 8–9

QFlowPilot can coordinate the workload, profiler, policy, execution adapter and validator.

Success condition: One command runs the complete workflow, with ideal Aer, noisy Aer and eventually a real IBM QPU adapter.

Milestone 5 — Research-ready system

After Stage 10

QFlowPilot can run repeatable experiments and compare policies across workloads.

Success condition: It generates reproducible CSV/JSON results and supports all six planned experiments.